CAMSV capture driver for MT6895 (Redmi K50 / rubens)
===================================================

cam_cap.c is a self-contained CAMSV capture driver for the camera sensors of
the MT6895 (Redmi K50, codename rubens).

Capture path
------------

  sensor -> CSI-2 port N -> SENINF intf 2N -> SENINF mux 1 -> CAM_MUX 3
         -> camsv1@1a110000 IMGO DMA -> V4L2 /dev/video0 (YUYV 422)

The default configuration captures the IMX582 rear camera (CSI port 2, SENINF
intf 4).  The port is a runtime parameter, so the same driver serves the other
sensors of this board as well:

  sensor    CSI port   SENINF intf   D-PHY_TOP      output
  IMX582    2          4             0x11c86000     4000x3000 / 2000x1500
  IMX596    0          0             0x11c82000     2592x1952   (front)
  S5K4H7    1          2             0x11c92000     3264x2448   (ultrawide)
  GC02M1    3          6             0x11c96000     1600x1200   (macro)

All of them are routed through CAM_MUX 3 into the single CAMSV instance this
driver owns, so one sensor can stream at a time.  Physical CSI port N maps to
SENINF interface 2N (only whole ports are supported by the SoC); the receiver
window is selected with dphy_base and the sensor with route_intf, route_mux,
cammux, i2c_bus and i2c_addr.

  insmod cam_cap.ko v4l2_enable=1 route_intf=0 dphy_base=0x11c82000 \
          exp_hsize=2592 exp_vsize=1952 out_width=2592 out_height=1952 \
          i2c_bus=8 i2c_addr=0x10

The driver owns that sensor's I2C bus for runtime exposure, gain and open-loop
DW9800V focus writes, programs SENINF / CAM_MUX / CAMSV, allocates the capture
buffer with dma_alloc_coherent() from CMA and maps it through the display
IOMMU.  Raw frames rotate through a three-slot capture pipeline, so the arm
thread does not wait for the converter to release the previous slot; conversion
itself runs on a worker pool whose threads are niced so the pool cannot starve
the rest of the system.  Each worker invalidates the cache lines of its own row
band (sync_parallel, default on) instead of having the converter thread
invalidate the whole frame first.  A per-frame governor on the conversion
thread drives AE and AWB; /proc/camcap exposes statistics and commands.

Sensor modes
------------

imx582_modes.h carries five IMX582 mode tables (preview 4000x3000,
normal_video 4000x2256, custom3 4000x2256@60, custom2 1920x1080@120,
hs_video 1920x1080@240).  The file is generated by scripts/gen_modes_header.py
in the companion repository listed below.  The driver replays a table itself
when the mode changes:

  echo modes > /proc/camcap                  list the tables
  echo "mode custom2 1" > /proc/camcap       switch to 1920x1080, binning 1

The same switch is reachable from V4L2:

  v4l2-ctl -d /dev/video0 --set-fmt-video=width=1920,height=1080,pixelformat=YUYV
  v4l2-ctl -d /dev/video0 --set-parm=240

VIDIOC_ENUM_FRAMESIZES and VIDIOC_ENUM_FRAMEINTERVALS report the tables; the
frame rate selects between tables of one size.  Modes that change the MIPI data
rate (custom3 and hs_video run at 1964 Mbps/lane, the others at 1370) need the
CSI receiver retimed.  The driver recomputes D-PHY HS_TRAIL and CSI2 DMY_CYCLE,
the two fields of the receiver setup that follow the rate (rx_rate=1).

The boot-time power-up sequence and the initial mode come from the bring-up
scripts in the companion repository; sensors whose controller is not present in
the board device tree (the macro camera sits on i2c@11d03000) additionally need
the runtime overlay that repository ships.

Enable and load
---------------

  CONFIG_VIDEO_MTK_CAMSV=m

  insmod cam_cap.ko v4l2_enable=1 pipe_slots=3 pipeline=1

conv_threads defaults to 8 (the workers are niced to 10 and the pool only runs
while a frame is converted), pipe_slots selects the capture pipeline depth
(2..4, default 3; three measured fastest).  The converter is compiled with -O3
for the same reason.  sync_parallel (default 1) switches between the per-band
and the whole-frame cache maintenance.

Useful parameters: v4l2_bin (1 = full size output, 2 = 2x2 binned),
v4l2_full_cache, exp_hsize/exp_vsize/v4l2_src_stride, exp_max, out_width/
out_height, rb_swap, pipeline, pipe_slots, conv_threads, sync_parallel,
route_intf/route_mux/cammux/dphy_base/seninf_base, i2c_bus/i2c_addr, rx_rate,
mode_trace, mode_init_replay, ae_enable/awb_enable/af_enable.  See the module's
MODULE_PARM_DESC strings.

Reproducible builds
-------------------

The module the phone runs reports

  srcversion: 493F61FF760E440C2CC5AA7
  vermagic:   7.2.0-g0b8dd2e87b3d-dirty SMP preempt mod_unload aarch64

The companion repository holds the kernel revision as the annotated tag
k50-camera-base (0b8dd2e87b3d), the kernel configuration and a one-shot script
that reproduces both values without a full kernel build.  See
docs/REPRODUCIBLE_BUILD.md there.

Notes, mode tables and bring-up scripts
---------------------------------------

  https://github.com/rubens-mt6895-mainline/k50-camera

The sensor register tables the init sequence is based on come from the vendor
kernel (MiCode/Xiaomi_Kernel_OpenSource, rubens-s-oss branch) for the IMX582 and
from the GC02M1 / IMX596 / S5K4H7 driver series under review in the upstream
repository for the other three sensors.  The four-camera bring-up and the
measurements are documented in docs/V4L2_CAMERA.md in the companion repository.

Design credit for the multi-sensor port mapping and the CSI receiver bring-up
goes to the PR that added the other three sensor drivers; the SENINF interface
numbering (physical port N -> interface 2N, TOP_PHY_CTRL_CSI) follows the
mtk_seninf71 driver in that series.
